# Valgrind memcheck suppressions for the Free Pascal runtime library (FPC 3.2.2,
# x86_64). Pass with --suppressions=scripts/valgrind/fpc-rtl.supp; the
# scheduled memory-safety job in .github/workflows/fuzz.yml does.
#
# IndexByte and IndexWord (rtl/x86_64/x86_64.inc) scan with aligned 16-byte
# SSE2 loads (movdqa), compare the whole block (pcmpeqb/pcmpeqw, pmovmskb) and
# branch on any match before they drop a match at or past the length. The last
# load reaches past the end of the buffer; it stays inside one aligned 16-byte
# block, so it never crosses into another page and cannot fault, and bytes past
# the end never decide the result. Memcheck still reports the branch when those
# bytes are undefined, for example the slack after a string's terminator in a
# block FPC's heap manager reused without telling Valgrind (#1776).
#
# What this gives up: only these two routines as the faulting frame, and only
# the branch, are suppressed. Undefined bytes inside the searched range are
# hidden too when the search finds nothing, because the "not found" result is
# a constant and memcheck does not follow definedness through a branch; any
# other read of those bytes (a copy that is later branched on, a comparison,
# a hash, output) still reports.
{
   fpc-rtl-indexbyte-aligned-overread
   Memcheck:Cond
   fun:SYSTEM_$$_INDEXBYTE$*
}
{
   fpc-rtl-indexword-aligned-overread
   Memcheck:Cond
   fun:SYSTEM_$$_INDEXWORD$*
}
